+ A.1 The fixed-reference phase space process Proof of the well-posedness assertion in Proposition 3.1 . Fix y,\widetilde{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} . In the notation of ( 3.4 ), the bounce rate ( 3.9 ) reads \lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(x,p)=\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)]_{+}\,. The deterministic harmonic flow, namely the solution ( 4.5 ) of ( 3.8 ), and the bounce map are \displaystyle\Phi_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}(x,p) \displaystyle\coloneqq\bigl(c_{y,\widetilde{x}}+(x-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\cos t+\sqrt{\eta}\,p\sin t,\;-\eta^{-1/2}(x-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\sin t+p\cos t\bigr)\,, \displaystyle\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}(x,p) \displaystyle\coloneqq(x,R_{h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)}p)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptR_{0}\coloneqq I\,. The bounce map is an involution. Although there is a possible discontinuity at h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)=0 , this creates no ambiguity because the event rate vanishes there. Let (E_{n})_{n\geq 1} be independent \operatorname{Exp}(1) random variables. Starting from \zeta_{0}\coloneqq(x_{0},p_{0}) at time T_{0}\coloneqq 0 , define recursively \displaystyle S_{n+1} \displaystyle\coloneqq\inf\Bigl\{u\geq 0:\int_{0}^{u}\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\Phi_{s}^{y,\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{T_{n}}))\,\mathrm{d}s\geq E_{n+1}\Bigr\}\,, (A.1) \displaystyle T_{n+1} \displaystyle\coloneqq T_{n}+S_{n+1}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\zeta_{T_{n+1}}\coloneqq\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}\bigl(\Phi_{S_{n+1}}^{y,\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{T_{n}})\bigr)\,, (A.2) when S_{n+1}<\infty . If the set is empty, set T_{n+1}=S_{n+1}=\infty and stop the recursion. Between jumps, set \zeta_{T_{n}+t}=\Phi_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{T_{n}}) for 0\leq t<S_{n+1} . To prove non-explosion, define the shifted harmonic energy H_{y,\widetilde{x}}(x,p)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl\{\eta^{-1}\,\|x-c_{y,\widetilde{x}}\|^{2}+\|p\|^{2}\bigr\}\,. The flow and each bounce preserve this energy. Hence, with \mathcal{E}\coloneqq H_{y,\widetilde{x}}(x_{0},p_{0}) , every state produced by the recursion satisfies \|p\|\leq\sqrt{2\mathcal{E}}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|x-c_{y,\widetilde{x}}\|\leq\sqrt{2\eta\mathcal{E}}\,. Because \nabla V is \beta -Lipschitz, \lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(x,p)\leq\bar{\lambda}_{\mathcal{E}}(y,\widetilde{x}) throughout the path, where \bar{\lambda}_{\mathcal{E}}(y,\widetilde{x})\coloneqq\sqrt{\eta}\,\beta\sqrt{2\mathcal{E}}\,\bigl\{\sqrt{2\eta\mathcal{E}}+\|c_{y,\widetilde{x}}-\widetilde{x}\|\bigr\}\,. Hence, \int_{0}^{u}\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\Phi_{s}^{y,\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{T_{n}}))\,\mathrm{d}s\leq\bar{\lambda}_{\mathcal{E}}(y,\widetilde{x})\,u\,. If \bar{\lambda}_{\mathcal{E}}(y,\widetilde{x})=0 , the event rate vanishes and there are no jumps. Otherwise, ( A.1 ) implies, for every finite waiting time, S_{n+1}\geq\frac{E_{n+1}}{\bar{\lambda}_{\mathcal{E}}(y,\widetilde{x})}\,. If the recursion does not terminate, it follows that T_{n}\geq\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{n}E_{j}}{\bar{\lambda}_{\mathcal{E}}(y,\widetilde{x})}\longrightarrow\infty almost surely by the strong law of large numbers. Thus, the jump times cannot accumulate, and the recursive construction defines a unique process for all t\geq 0 . Finally, the memoryless property of the exponential clocks gives the time-homogeneous Markov property. This is precisely the integrated hazard construction of Davis [ 12 , Section 2] . ∎ With the process now constructed, we prove the stationarity assertion in Proposition 3.1 and record the stronger operator facts used to analyze the averaged kernel. Recall \nu_{\eta,y} from ( 3.1 ), and define momentum reversal by \mathsf{R}(x,p)\coloneqq(x,-p)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt(\mathsf{R}F)(x,p)\coloneqq F(\mathsf{R}(x,p))\,. As usual, we use the same symbol for \nu_{\eta,y} and its Lebesgue density: \nu_{\eta,y}(x,p)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V_{y}(x)-\frac{\|p\|^{2}}{2}\Bigr\}\,. Proposition A.1 (Path reversal, invariance, and the L^{2} semigroup) . Let (\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}})_{t\geq 0} be the Markov transition operators of the process constructed in Proposition 3.1 . For every t\geq 0 , (\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}})^{*}=\mathsf{R}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{R}\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }L^{2}(\nu_{\eta,y})\,. (A.3) Consequently: (i) \nu_{\eta,y} is invariant; (ii) (\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}})_{t\geq 0} is a strongly continuous contraction semigroup on L^{2}(\nu_{\eta,y}) ; (iii) if \mathsf{\Pi}_{p}:L^{2}(\nu_{\eta,y})\to L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}) denotes Gaussian momentum averaging, then \mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*} is a self-adjoint Markov contraction on L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}) . Idea of the proof. The identity ( A.3 ) is a momentum-reversed form of detailed balance. To see the mechanism formally, write \mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{x}}=\mathcal{A}+\mathcal{J} , where \displaystyle\mathcal{A}f(x,p) \displaystyle\coloneqq\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\frac{(x-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})^{\top}}{\sqrt{\eta}}\nabla_{p}f(x,p), \displaystyle\mathcal{J}f(x,p) \displaystyle\coloneqq\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(x,p)\bigl\{f(\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}(x,p))-f(x,p)\bigr\}. Momentum reversal reverses the harmonic flow, so \mathsf{R}\mathcal{A}\mathsf{R}=-\mathcal{A} . Moreover, \mathcal{A}\log\nu_{\eta,y}(x,p)=-\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)=-\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(x,p)+\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(x,-p). Formal integration by parts gives \mathcal{A}^{*}=-\mathcal{A}+\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}-\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}\circ\mathsf{R}. The bounce map \mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}} is a \nu_{\eta,y} -preserving involution, commutes with \mathsf{R} , and satisfies \lambda_{\widetilde{x}}\circ\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}=\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}\circ\mathsf{R} . A formal change of variables therefore gives \mathcal{J}^{*}G=(\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}\circ\mathsf{R})(G\circ\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}})-\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}G. Combining the two identities yields \mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{x}}^{*}G=-\mathcal{A}G+(\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}\circ\mathsf{R})(G\circ\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}-G)=(\mathsf{R}\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{R})G. Formally exponentiating this identity gives (\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}})^{*}=\mathsf{R}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{R} ; Gaussian momentum averaging then removes \mathsf{R} and produces an ordinary self-adjoint position kernel. This generator calculation is only heuristic as stated, as the standard arguments are not immediately applicable because \mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}} may be discontinuous where h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)=0 . The pathwise argument below proves the same identity without requiring differentiability or continuity of the bounce map. Proof. Along \zeta_{s}=(x_{s},p_{s})=\Phi_{s}^{y,\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{0}) , \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s}\log\nu_{\eta,y}(\zeta_{s})=-\sqrt{\eta}\,p_{s}^{\top}h_{\widetilde{x}}(x_{s})=-\bigl\{\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{s})-\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\mathsf{R}\zeta_{s})\bigr\}\,. (A.4) Moreover, by definition of \mathsf{R} and \Phi_{t} , \mathsf{R}\Phi_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{R}=\Phi_{-t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{R}\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}=\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{R}\,. If \zeta^{+}=\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}\zeta^{-} is a non-trivial jump, then h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)\neq 0 , (p^{-})^{\top}h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)>0 , and \lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\zeta^{-})=\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\mathsf{R}\zeta^{+})\,. (A.5) The flow and \mathsf{R} preserve Lebesgue measure, as does the possibly discontinuous bounce map: by Fubini’s theorem and orthogonality, for every integrable F , \int F(\mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}}(x,p))\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}p=\int F(x,p)\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}p\,. (A.6) To pass from these identities to complete trajectories, let \Delta_{n}(t)\coloneqq\{(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}):0<t_{1}<\cdots<t_{n}<t\}\,. Given \zeta_{0} and \mathbf{t}\in\Delta_{n}(t) , let \zeta_{s}^{\zeta_{0},\mathbf{t}} be the path obtained by following \Phi^{y,\widetilde{x}} and applying \mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}} at the prescribed times. Iterating the exponential clock construction gives, for every bounded measurable functional \Psi of the path up to time t , \displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\zeta_{0}}\Psi(\zeta_{[0,t]}) \displaystyle=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\int_{\Delta_{n}(t)}\Psi(\zeta_{[0,t]}^{\zeta_{0},\mathbf{t}})\exp\Bigl\{-\!\int_{0}^{t}\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{s}^{\zeta_{0},\mathbf{t}})\,\mathrm{d}s\Bigr\}\prod_{j=1}^{n}\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{t_{j}-}^{\zeta_{0},\mathbf{t}})\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{t}\,. (A.7) The series is absolutely convergent because \lvert\Delta_{n}(t)\rvert=t^{n}/n! and the rate is bounded by \bar{\lambda}_{\mathcal{E}}(y,\widetilde{x}) . Integrate ( A.7 ) against \nu_{\eta,y}(\mathrm{d}\zeta_{0}) and apply (\zeta_{s})_{0\leq s\leq t}\longmapsto(\mathsf{R}\zeta_{t-s})_{0\leq s\leq t}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})\longmapsto(t-t_{n},\ldots,t-t_{1})\,. (A.8) For completeness, we justify this change of variables despite the possible discontinuity of the bounce map. For fixed \mathbf{t}\in\Delta_{n}(t) , the endpoint map \zeta_{0}\mapsto\zeta_{t}^{\zeta_{0},\mathbf{t}} is a finite composition of the Borel involution \mathsf{S}_{\widetilde{x}} and the affine harmonic flows \Phi_{u}^{y,\widetilde{x}} . By ( A.6 ) and the explicit flow formula, each factor is a measure-preserving Borel bijection. Hence the endpoint map, and therefore \zeta_{0}\mapsto\mathsf{R}\zeta_{t}^{\zeta_{0},\mathbf{t}} , is a Borel measure-preserving bijection. The identities preceding ( A.7 ) show that its inverse is obtained by using the reversed ordered times in ( A.8 ). The map on \Delta_{n}(t) is linear with absolute determinant one. Thus the full change of variables is measure preserving; no Jacobian is needed. Integrating ( A.4 ) over the deterministic arcs and using that a reflection preserves \nu_{\eta,y} gives \nu_{\eta,y}(\zeta_{0})\,e^{-\int_{0}^{t}\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\zeta_{s})\,\mathrm{d}s}=\nu_{\eta,y}(\zeta_{t})\,e^{-\int_{0}^{t}\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\mathsf{R}\zeta_{s})\,\mathrm{d}s}\,. This identity matches the initial density and survival factor of a forward path with those of its reversal, while ( A.5 ) matches every jump-rate factor. Hence, each n -jump term in ( A.7 ) is unchanged by ( A.8 ). Tonelli’s theorem justifies the termwise change of variables for non-negative bounded functions; decomposing signed functions into positive and negative parts then gives, for bounded measurable F,G , \int F\,\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}G\,\mathrm{d}\nu_{\eta,y}=\int(\mathsf{R}G)\,\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}(\mathsf{R}F)\,\mathrm{d}\nu_{\eta,y}\,. Taking F=1 proves invariance, and the identity extends by density to ( A.3 ) on L^{2}(\nu_{\eta,y}) . Invariance and Jensen’s inequality give \|\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}F\|_{2}\leq\|F\|_{2} . It remains to prove strong continuity. For F\in C_{\rm c}(\mathbb{R}^{2d}) , the deterministic flow converges to the identity as t\searrow 0 , and the probability of a jump before time t is 1-\exp\Bigl\{-\int_{0}^{t}\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(\Phi_{s}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\zeta)\,\mathrm{d}s\Bigr\}\longrightarrow 0\,. Dominated convergence therefore gives \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}F\to F in L^{2} . Density of C_{\rm c}(\mathbb{R}^{2d}) and contractivity extend this to all F\in L^{2} . Finally, Gaussian momentum averaging satisfies \mathsf{R}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*} and \mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{R}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p} . Combining these identities with ( A.3 ) gives (\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*})^{*}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}(\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}})^{*}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{R}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{R}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}\,. This proves self-adjointness; the Markov and contraction properties are inherited from \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}} . ∎ 
runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-half-turn-construction-preread73/discover-v2.stdout.log:15: trailing whitespace.
+ A.2 The averaged ideal half-turn kernel Proof of Proposition 3.2 . Fix y . For each reference point \widetilde{x} , Proposition A.1 shows that the fixed-reference position operator \mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{\pi}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*} is a self-adjoint Markov contraction on L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}) . The reference point in Algorithm 1 is drawn independently from \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} . Consequently, the conditional half-turn operator is the average (\mathsf{H}_{y}f)(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\bigl(\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{\pi}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}f\bigr)(x)\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}\widetilde{x})\,. The averaging measure does not depend on x , so \mathsf{H}_{y} is again a self-adjoint Markov contraction. In kernel form, this is the detailed-balance identity \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\,\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})=\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\,\mathcal{H}_{y}(x^{\prime},\mathrm{d}x)\,, which proves the proposition. ∎ The preceding reversibility statement holds for each fixed y . To use these kernels as a single operator on the augmented space, we next verify their joint measurability and then integrate the conditional operator identities over y . Proposition A.2 (Joint measurability and augmented space half-turn operator) . The family of conditional half-turn kernels (\mathcal{H}_{y})_{y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}} admits a version, again denoted by \mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}) , such that (y,x)\mapsto\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,A) is Borel measurable for every Borel set A\subset\mathbb{R}^{d} . Consequently, (\mathsf{H}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x^{\prime},y)\,\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}) (A.9) defines a self-adjoint Markov contraction on L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) . Proof. Since \nabla V is continuous, the harmonic flow, event rate, and reflection map are jointly Borel; the convention R_{0}=I ensures this even where the reflection normal vanishes. The integrated hazard is jointly Borel, with continuous, nondecreasing dependence on time, so its threshold-crossing times are Borel, as can be checked using rational times. Thus the recursion ( A.1 )–( A.2 ) makes each jump time and post-jump state Borel in (y,x_{0},p_{0},\widetilde{x},(E_{j})_{j\geq 1}) . By non-explosion, the terminal state at time \pi is the almost-sure limit of the Borel terminal states obtained by allowing at most n jumps. Setting the limit to zero wherever it does not exist gives a Borel version. The conditional density in ( 2.8 ) is jointly Borel in (y,\widetilde{x}) . Integrating \mathbf{1}_{\{x_{\pi}\in A\}} against this reference law and the laws of the Gaussian momentum and exponential clocks therefore makes (y,x)\mapsto\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,A) Borel for every Borel set A . Finally, each \mathsf{H}_{y} is a self-adjoint Markov contraction by Proposition 3.2 . Integrating the conditional contraction bound gives \displaystyle\|\mathsf{H}f\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})}^{2} \displaystyle\leq\int\bigl\|f(\cdot,y)\bigr\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y})}^{2}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)=\|f\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})}^{2}\,. Conditional self-adjointness and Fubini likewise give \langle f,\mathsf{H}g\rangle=\langle\mathsf{H}f,g\rangle for bounded measurable f,g , and hence for all L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) by density. Positivity and \mathsf{H}1=1 follow from the corresponding properties of each \mathsf{H}_{y} . ∎ 
