+ A.3 Bounce complexity and capped-rate implementation Proposition 3.3 follows immediately from the following lemma. More specifically, for every fixed y,\widetilde{x} , Proposition A.1 shows that the phase space process preserves \nu_{\eta,y} . Hence, under the initialization in the proposition and conditional on Y=y , the triple (x_{t},p_{t},\widetilde{X}) has law \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)\otimes\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} at every time t\in[0,\pi] . Lemma A.3 (Tail bound for the event rate) . Let X,\widetilde{X}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\rm iid}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} and P\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I) be independent, and set \lambda\coloneqq\sqrt{\eta}\,[P^{\top}(\nabla V(X)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X}))]_{+} . For every u\geq 0 , \mathbb{P}\bigl\{\lambda>C\beta\eta\,(\sqrt{du}+u)\bigr\}\leq Ce^{-u}\,. (A.10) Consequently, for \ell\geq 0 and \bar{\lambda}_{\ell}=C\beta\eta\,(\sqrt{d\ell}+\ell) , \mathbb{E}(\lambda-\bar{\lambda}_{\ell})_{+}\leq C\beta\eta\sqrt{d}\,e^{-c\ell}\,. (A.11) Proof. The measure \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} is (\alpha+\eta^{-1}) -strongly log-concave, and therefore ( D.7 ) gives \mathbb{P}\bigl\{\|X-\mathbb{E}X\|\geq C\sqrt{\eta\,(d+u)}\bigr\}\leq e^{-u} for every u\geq 0 . Therefore, \mathbb{P}\bigl\{\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\nabla V(X)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})\|>2C\beta\eta\sqrt{d+u}\bigr\}\leq 2e^{-u}\,. (A.12) Conditional on h=\sqrt{\eta}\,\{\nabla V(X)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})\} , the scalar P^{\top}h\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\|h\|^{2}) . The Gaussian tail and a union bound with ( A.12 ) therefore show that, for u\geq 1 and outside an event of probability at most 2e^{-u} , [P^{\top}h]_{+}\leq C\sqrt{u}\,\|h\|\leq C\beta\eta\,(\sqrt{du}+u)\,, which proves ( A.10 ) in this range. The bound for 0\leq u<1 follows after increasing C accordingly. Finally, ( A.11 ) follows by integration. ∎ Proof of Proposition 4.2 . For part (i), first suppose that (X_{0},Y)\sim\pi_{\eta} , and write \mathbb{P}_{\pi_{\eta}} and \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}} for probability and expectation under the resulting coupled construction, including the reference point, initial momentum, and Poisson random measure shared by the ideal and capped processes. Let \mathcal{D} be the event that the two paths separate. The paths agree until the first event from the excess rate (\lambda_{t}-\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta})_{+} , so the probability of \mathcal{D} is bounded by the expected number of such events: \mathbb{P}_{\pi_{\eta}}(\mathcal{D})\leq\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}\int_{0}^{\pi}(\lambda_{t}-\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta})_{+}\,\mathrm{d}t\,. By stationarity, ( A.11 ) applies at every t . Since the small-scale assumption implies \beta\eta\sqrt{d}\leq c_{\rm evt} , we obtain \mathbb{P}_{\pi_{\eta}}(\mathcal{D})\leq Ce^{-c\ell_{\xi,\Delta}}\leq e^{-\Delta}\xi^{2}\,. The definition of \ell_{\xi,\Delta} in ( 4.6 ) gives the last inequality after increasing K_{\rm evt} . Now let \nu_{0} satisfy D_{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\leq\Delta , and set h_{0}=\mathrm{d}\nu_{0}/\mathrm{d}\pi_{\eta} . Relative to the coupled path law \mathbb{P}_{\pi_{\eta}} , the construction started from \nu_{0} has likelihood ratio h_{0}(X_{0},Y) : all reference points, momenta, and Poisson variables are generated by the same conditional rules. Hence, Cauchy–Schwarz gives \mathbb{P}_{\nu_{0}}(\mathcal{D})=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[h_{0}(X_{0},Y)\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{D}}]\leq(\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}h_{0}^{2})^{1/2}\,\mathbb{P}_{\pi_{\eta}}(\mathcal{D})^{1/2}\leq e^{\Delta/2}\,(e^{-\Delta}\xi^{2})^{1/2}=\xi\,. This proves part (i). For part (ii), the number of arrivals of the rate- \bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta} proposal clock before time \pi is Poisson with mean \pi\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta} . Since the reference gradient is already cached, the capped simulation makes exactly one gradient query at each arrival. This proves ( 4.7 ). ∎ 
